A model conversion graph relates Australian dollars $d$ to British pounds $p$. Find the gradient as a fraction, write the equation relating $p$ and $d$, and explain what the gradient represents.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{mathblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[gridgrey,very thin,xstep=9mm,ystep=9mm] (0,0) grid (6,4);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(6.5,0);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,4.5);
\path (3,-0.85) node {Australian dollars $d$};
\path (-1.05,2) node[rotate=90] {British pounds $p$};
\foreach \x in {1,...,6} {\draw[black] (\x,0)--(\x,-0.08);\path (\x,-0.12) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {1,...,4} {\draw[black] (0,\y)--(-0.08,\y);\path (-0.12,\y) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\y$};}
\path (0,0) node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$0$};
\draw[mathblue,thick] (0,0)--(6,4);
\fill[black] (6,4) circle (1.8pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The graph is a straight line through the origin. Use $(0,0)$ and $(6,4)$.

$\begin{aligned} k&=\frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}}\\&=\frac{4-0}{6-0}\\&=\frac{2}{3}\end{aligned}$

The equation is $p=\frac{2}{3}d$.

The gradient represents $\frac{2}{3}$ of a British pound per Australian dollar in this model.

